>I would be interested to hear from someone with a background in nutrition/
>medicine/physiology/biology, however. It seems to me that, in real life, it 
>would be next to IMPOSSIBLE for someone to kill themselves by over-feeding, 
>even if it was voluntary. Wouldn't they just get slower, and SLOWER, and 
>S-L-O-W-E-R and finally they would just stop and/or pass out? 

You don't need to be a doctor to know that people *can* kill themselves by
overeating. In Bavaria, Germany, there are regular eating competitions.
People compete for who can eat the largest number of Weisswurst (a soft
pork sausage), potato balls, eggs, etc. in a given amount of time.

Every few years, someone dies at these competitions, usually from heart
failure (all the food exerts a lot of pressure on internal organs), or
from a ruptured stomach, which causes a lot of internal bleeding.

